<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lațcu of Moldavia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lațcu was prince of Moldavia from about 1367 to about 1375, the son of Bogdan I. He sought a Catholic bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siret">Siret</a>, established around 1371, to win papal support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was succeeded by Petru I Mușat, who did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1387.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latcu-of-moldavia.jpg" alt="Lațcu of Moldavia" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Moldova · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_Moldova_196.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Lațcu succeeded his father, Bogdan I, who had won Moldavia's independence from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a>. Once Louis inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from his uncle Casimir III in 1370, he could press Moldavia from the east and the north. The contemporary chronicler John of Küküllő wrote that the voivodes of Moldavia accepted Louis's suzerainty, but gave no date, and the historian Dennis Deletant finds nothing to suggest that Hungarian overlordship was restored. Lațcu seems, however, to have accepted it in his last years.</p><p>In 1369 Lațcu became a Roman Catholic. Early in 1370 he sent two Franciscan friars of Polish origin to tell Pope Urban V of his conversion and to ask for a diocese at his seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siret">Siret</a>. Deletant argues that his motives were mainly political, since he approached the Holy See directly rather than through Hungarian prelates. The pope had three prelates, among them Jan Očko of Vlašim, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, examine the matter, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi">Pope Gregory XI</a> authorised the consecration of the Polish Andrzej Jastrzębiec as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Siret, his diocese answering directly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The pope styled Lațcu duke of the Moldavian parts. His wife Ana and daughter Anastasia stayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 14 March 1372 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, acknowledged Louis I's rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and other lands, Moldavia among them. The historian J. Sýkora suggests that Lațcu had sought an alliance with the emperor against Louis, Victor Spinei that he used peaceful relations with Louis to extend his rule toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and Ioan-Aurel Pop that Louis prepared an expedition against him in 1374. The exact date of his death is unknown. He was buried beside his father in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery at Rădăuți, and his daughter Anastasia married Roman I of Moldavia and was the mother of Alexander I of Moldavia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lațcu of Moldavia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
